---
/**
 * The formulary's ingredient vocabulary, resolved.
 *
 * WHY THIS PAGE EXISTS AND WHY IT IS SHAPED LIKE A TABLE. The formulary names its ingredients in
 * Sanskrit and uses several names for the same drug: 1,973 ingredient rows across 638 distinct
 * spellings. A label names the same drug in Hindi or by binomial. Nothing published connects the
 * two, which means the official standard for a classical preparation cannot be mechanically
 * checked by anyone who does not already read Sanskrit pharmacognosy.
 *
 * The measured reason for the shape: this site's own panel found long-form guides earn no
 * citations while precisely-named things set out as structured fact do. "What is Marica" and "what
 * does Nagara mean in a formulary entry" are precisely-named questions, so each name is a row with
 * its answer and its evidence beside it, and the ambiguous and unresolved ones are rows too.
 */
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import crosswalk from '../data/afi-crosswalk.json';
import { abs, rel } from '../lib/site';

const data = crosswalk as any;
const s = data.summary;
const vocab = (data.vocabulary as any[]);

const title = 'What the formulary\'s ingredient names mean';
const description = `The ${s.distinctNames} Sanskrit ingredient names used across the 101 Ayurvedic `
  + `Formulary of India entries transcribed here, resolved to a botanical identity where one could `
  + `be established. ${s.namesAmbiguous} names the formulary uses for more than one drug are marked `
  + `ambiguous; ${s.namesUnresolved} are published as unresolved rather than guessed.`;
const canonical = '/crosswalk/';

const n = (x: number) => x.toLocaleString('en-GB');
const resolved = vocab.filter((v) => v.status === 'resolved');
const ambiguous = vocab.filter((v) => v.status === 'ambiguous');
const unresolved = vocab.filter((v) => v.status === 'unresolved');
const plantGaps = unresolved.filter((v) => v.substanceClass === 'plant');

const jsonld = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Dataset',
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#dataset`,
    name: data.name,
    description: data.description,
    url: abs(canonical),
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    isAccessibleForFree: true,
    creator: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${abs('/')}#organization` },
    variableMeasured: ['formulary ingredient name', 'printed gloss', 'botanical identity', 'substance class', 'basis of resolution'],
    distribution: [
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'JSON', encodingFormat: 'application/json', contentUrl: abs('/afi-crosswalk.json') },
      { '@type': 'DataDownload', name: 'CSV', encodingFormat: 'text/csv', contentUrl: abs('/afi-crosswalk.csv') },
    ],
  },
  /**
   * Each resolved name as a DefinedTerm carrying the botanical identity, so the answer to "what is
   * Marica" is in the markup and not only in a table cell. The ambiguous and unresolved names are
   * deliberately not emitted as DefinedTerms: a term whose definition this project does not have
   * should not be asserted as defined.
   */
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'DefinedTermSet',
    '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#vocabulary`,
    name: 'Ayurvedic Formulary of India ingredient names',
    description: 'Sanskrit ingredient names as the formulary prints them, each resolved to a botanical identity.',
    url: abs(canonical),
    license: 'https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/',
    hasDefinedTerm: resolved.map((v) => ({
      '@type': 'DefinedTerm',
      name: v.name,
      description: `${v.name} in the Ayurvedic Formulary of India is ${v.resolvesTo[0].binomial ?? 'a drug this project holds a monograph for'}`
        + `, used in ${v.formularyRows} ingredient row${v.formularyRows === 1 ? '' : 's'}.`,
      ...(v.resolvesTo[0].binomial ? { alternateName: v.resolvesTo[0].binomial } : {}),
      url: abs(v.resolvesTo[0].monograph),
      inDefinedTermSet: { '@id': `${abs(canonical)}#vocabulary` },
    })),
  },
];
---
<Page title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} updated={data.updatedAt}
  trail={[{ label: 'Formulary ingredient names' }]} jsonld={jsonld}>
  <p class="answer">
    The Ayurvedic Formulary of India names its ingredients in Sanskrit, and it does not use one name
    per drug. Across the 101 entries transcribed on this site, <strong>{n(s.ingredientRows)} ingredient
    rows use {n(s.distinctNames)} distinct spellings</strong>. <i>Abhaya</i> and <i>Pathya</i> are both
    haritaki. <i>Nagara</i> is sunthi. <i>Marica</i> is black pepper. This resolves each name to a
    botanical identity where one could be established, and says plainly where one could not.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    A label names its ingredients in Hindi or by binomial; the formulary names them in Sanskrit.
    Without a crosswalk between the two, the official standard for a classical preparation cannot be
    checked by anyone who does not already read Sanskrit pharmacognosy. That is what this is for.
  </p>

  <table class="facts">
    <caption>What is here</caption>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Ingredient rows covered</th><td>{n(s.ingredientRows)}, across 101 formulary entries</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Distinct names</th><td>{n(s.distinctNames)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Resolved to a botanical identity</th><td>{n(s.namesResolved)} names, {n(s.rowsResolved)} rows</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Of the plant rows</th><td>{n(s.plantRowsResolved)} of {n(s.plantRows)}</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Resolved by a gloss the book itself prints</th><td>{n(s.rowsResolvedViaAPrintedGloss)} rows</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Used for more than one drug</th><td>{n(s.namesAmbiguous)} names, marked ambiguous rather than collapsed</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Unresolved</th><td>{n(s.namesUnresolved)} names, published as unresolved rather than guessed</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>How a name was resolved, and why that is stated on every row</h2>
  <p>
    The strongest evidence is the formulary's own: it prints a gloss against an ingredient on 722
    rows, so where it says <i>Abhaya (haritaki)</i> that is the book telling you which drug it
    means. Those glosses are used per row and never chained, because a gloss is true of the row it
    is printed on: an early version of this work chained them and produced
    <i>Madhuka = Madhu = Maksika</i> resolved to arjuna, merging liquorice, honey and a mineral
    calcine across 47 rows.
  </p>
  <p>
    Otherwise a name is matched against the herb monographs published here, which carry
    GBIF-resolved binomials. An exact name match wins outright and an approximate one is flagged:
    {n(s.rowsResolvedOnAnApproximateName)} rows rest on an approximate match. That distinction is
    not pedantry. <i>Pippali</i> is <i>Piper longum</i> and <i>Pippala</i> is <i>Ficus religiosa</i>,
    one vowel apart and unrelated, and a matcher built for product names called the most-used
    ingredient in the formulary a sacred fig across 40 rows before exact matches were made to win.
  </p>

  <h2>Names the formulary uses for more than one drug</h2>
  <p>
    Reported rather than resolved. These are facts about the vocabulary, and a crosswalk that
    silently picked one reading would be wrong a predictable share of the time while looking
    complete.
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Rows</th><th>Resolves to</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {ambiguous.map((v) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="term">{v.name}</td>
            <td>{v.formularyRows}</td>
            <td>
              {v.resolvesTo.map((r: any, i: number) => (
                <>{i > 0 && ' · '}<a href={rel(r.monograph)}>{r.binomial ?? r.monograph}</a> ({r.rows})</>
              ))}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Every resolved name</h2>
  <p>
    Ordered by how often the formulary uses it, because the commonest names are the ones a reader
    checking a label will meet first.
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Formulary name</th><th>Rows</th><th>Botanical identity</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {resolved.map((v) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="term">{v.name}</td>
            <td>{v.formularyRows}</td>
            <td><a href={rel(v.resolvesTo[0].monograph)}>{v.resolvesTo[0].binomial ?? v.resolvesTo[0].monograph}</a></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>What is still unresolved</h2>
  <p>
    {n(unresolved.length)} names, {n(plantGaps.length)} of them plant drugs this project holds no
    monograph for, and the rest vehicles, sweeteners, salts, fats and mineral preparations that have
    no botanical identity to resolve. They are listed so the gap is visible and someone can close it
    with a source.
  </p>
  <p class="termlist">
    {unresolved.map((v, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ' · '}<span>{v.name}</span>{v.formularyRows > 1 && <> ({v.formularyRows})</>}</>))}
  </p>

  <h2>Download</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={rel('/afi-crosswalk.json')}>afi-crosswalk.json</a> and
    <a href={rel('/afi-crosswalk.csv')}>afi-crosswalk.csv</a>, CC BY 4.0. The CSV is one row per
    ingredient row, {n(s.ingredientRows)} of them, carrying the formulation, the formulary entry
    number, the name as printed, the gloss the book printed beside it, what it resolves to and on
    what basis. That is the join anyone checking a label against the formulary actually needs.
  </p>

  <p class="meta">
    Built {data.updatedAt} from the formulary transcription and the monographs on this site, so it
    cannot drift from either. The formulary entries themselves are at <a href={rel('/afi/')}>/afi/</a>.
  </p>
</Page>

<style>
  .tablewrap th, .tablewrap td { text-align: left; padding: .4rem .7rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: .93rem; }
  .tablewrap td.term { font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
  .termlist { font-size: .92rem; color: var(--ink-soft); line-height: 1.8; }
</style>
